WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.712
The final activity asks you to combine the main ideas into one small PostgreSQL workflow.

00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:13.302
Begin with a SELECT using chosen columns, add a WHERE condition, and sort the result.

00:00:13.302 --> 00:00:19.227
Then insert one test row with RETURNING and verify that row with a key-based SELECT.

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:23.817
After that, update the row safely.

00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:29.897
If you are ready for more, the optional challenge adds a transaction with ROLLBACK, a JSONB query,

00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:35.827
an INSERT INTO SELECT pattern, and an explanation of a constraint error.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:40.062
Do not try to do every part at once.

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:47.187
Start from a pattern, change one piece, run it, inspect the result, and then add the next step.

00:00:47.187 --> 00:00:52.822
That incremental method is how SQL work becomes easier to debug and safer to maintain.

00:00:52.822 --> 00:01:00.412
Connect this idea to a data engineer moving semi-structured API payloads into PostgreSQL.

00:01:00.412 --> 00:01:06.057
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:15.942
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:22.362
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:22.362 --> 00:01:28.087
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
